# Pulled platform reference — NOT source, and deliberately never committed.
# `octwin platform-kb pull` writes this from whatever platform you are pointed at, so a
# committed copy is a snapshot that goes stale the moment the platform moves. The Octwin
# Claude plugin ships no snapshot for the same reason: the KB is pulled, not bundled.
.octwin/

# Content-addressed staging the CLI writes while validating/deploying.
.pack-bundles/

node_modules/

# Internal engineering feedback on the platform + CLI — a candid list of our own bugs,
# gaps and workarounds, written for the platform team. Useful locally, not something to
# publish under the company org on a public repo. Delete this line to include it.
FEEDBACK.md

# Per-developer CLI deploy config, NOT part of a pack.
#
# `pack.json` supplies `platform_url` (+ optional tenant/project pins) to `octwin deploy`, and it
# is excluded from the uploaded bundle by the CLI and from the artifact by the platform's own
# `classifyPackPath` — so the platform has never seen it. What a committed copy DID do was ship our
# dev machine's pins to everyone: every pack in this repo carried
# `http://localhost:3000` + workspace `test`, so a cloner running `octwin deploy` in a pack
# directory silently targeted a machine and a workspace that were not theirs.
#
# Keep yours locally; `octwin init` writes one, and `--url` / `PACK_PLATFORM_URL` cover the case
# where you have none.
pack.json
ENRICHMENT.md
BACKLOG.md
